<section>
    <h2 id="what-is-vaultex">What is Vaultex?</h2>
    <p>
        Vaultex is a hosted identity provider that gives your application three things out of the box:
        a sign-in screen, secure tokens, and a dashboard for managing users and roles. Customers run a
        single Vaultex instance and serve many tenants from it &mdash; each tenant gets isolated users,
        roles, policies, and audit logs.
    </p>

    <h3>Standards-first</h3>
    <p>
        Every Vaultex flow speaks <strong>OpenID Connect</strong> and <strong>OAuth 2.0</strong>. Your
        client libraries already know how to talk to it. There is no proprietary SDK to learn and no
        lock-in.
    </p>

    <h3>Multi-tenant by design</h3>
    <p>
        Tenants are first-class. A single Vaultex deployment can host any number of tenants, each
        with their own users, roles, branding, and admin team. Tenant data never leaks across the
        boundary &mdash; queries are filtered at the database layer.
    </p>

    <h3>Built for developers</h3>
    <p>
        Vaultex is opinionated where it should be (PKCE on by default, refresh-token rotation,
        short-lived access tokens) and stays out of your way everywhere else.
    </p>
</section>

<section>
    <h2 id="how-it-fits">How Vaultex fits into your stack</h2>
    <p>
        Most applications integrate Vaultex in one of two ways. Pick the model that matches your
        architecture:
    </p>

    <h3>Server-rendered web app</h3>
    <p>
        Your backend redirects unauthenticated users to Vaultex's authorize endpoint. After the user
        signs in, Vaultex sends them back with an authorization code. Your backend exchanges the code
        for tokens and stores the session in a cookie.
    </p>

    <h3>Single-page app + API</h3>
    <p>
        Your SPA performs the OAuth 2.0 authorization code flow with PKCE entirely in the browser.
        It receives an access token and sends it on every API call. Your API validates the token's
        signature on each request.
    </p>

    <div class="docs-callout docs-callout--info">
