<section>
    <h2 id="example">A 30-second example</h2>
    <p>Here's what a sign-in flow looks like from your application's perspective:</p>

    <div class="docs-codeblock">
        <div class="docs-codeblock-header">
            <span class="docs-codeblock-lang">C#</span>
            <button class="docs-codeblock-copy" onclick="vxCopy(this)">Copy</button>
        </div>
        <pre><code>// Program.cs
builder.Services.AddAuthentication(options =&gt;
{
    options.DefaultScheme = CookieAuthenticationDefaults.AuthenticationScheme;
    options.DefaultChallengeScheme = OpenIdConnectDefaults.AuthenticationScheme;
})
.AddCookie()
.AddOpenIdConnect(options =&gt;
{
    options.Authority   = "https://auth.vaultex.io";
    options.ClientId    = "your-client-id";
    options.ClientSecret = "your-client-secret";
    options.ResponseType = "code";
    options.UsePkce      = true;
    options.Scope.Add("openid");
    options.Scope.Add("profile");
});</code></pre>
    </div>

    <p>
        That's it. Your users now sign in through Vaultex, your app receives an ID token and an
        access token, and you can call protected APIs on behalf of the user.
    </p>
</section>

